## Implemented laws and actual route

The single native parser has a static contextual admission mode. Ordinary parsing still refuses assignment collisions and retains native translation rules; contextual admission preserves group boundaries and source facts needed by the compiler. The single iterative compiler gives graph context C, actual returned domain R, and immutable initial bindings I distinct identities. Assignment aliases are disjoint, deterministic, and hygienic even against caller variables resembling generated names. Initial values remain typed RDF terms, including language, direction, blank, and triple-term identity. They are never stringified.

Expression forgetting uses actual R and I according to source facts; an empty logical domain thaws prior C before overlaying I. Relational expressions have the genuine empty `_vars` fact. Executable EXISTS root Filter retains its context exception. Project/EXISTS dependencies travel through preparation, caching, and deferred execution. A primary-oracle correction was required: EXISTS executable graph data does not carry the outer translation's lazy-Join annotation. The compiler explicitly treats these executable joins as nonlazy; strict ordinary native Join is unchanged. Actual oracle inputs/results for this correction are retained in the context, retry/EXISTS clause-fact, and volatile receipts, and permanent executable tests retain the resulting law.

One boxed typed Apply policy supplies declared correlation masks and operator modes. There is one existing evaluator dispatcher, expression VM, scratch/storage/governor home, and each operator's shared kernel. OPTIONAL remembers the incoming solution for retry, stores its RHS once, uses left precedence, and stops remembered retry at its first accepted row. Callback evaluation order follows each yielded row through BIND/Filter/projection and into Group accumulation; RHS, first-pass condition, and retry effects interleave correctly. Nonlazy Join and MINUS materialize the logical RHS once while consuming the left generator lazily. DISTINCT and COUNT(DISTINCT *) use actual logical returned rows; contextual REDUCED removes adjacent duplicates. GROUP handles explicit/implicit empty groups, group-only HAVING, clause-local ORDER aggregate sampling, distinct eligibility followed by accepted update reevaluation, and input/aggregate continuation. GRAPH restores the outer active graph before delivering to downstream consumers and does not fabricate a bound graph variable removed by inner projection.

The sealed `PreparedRdflibQuery` route prepares the compiled algebra through the shared internal preparation home and executes through the shared prepared-view engine. Ordinary `prepare_algebra` and `PreparedQuery::rewritten` reject contextual Apply directly and inside EXISTS using their existing validation traversal. SHACL's prebinding admission explicitly rejects it. Raw evaluator entry and the hidden fixture helper are technically public and are not claimed to reject arbitrary caller-built algebra; neither applies ordinary preparation transformations to the sealed contextual handle. Policy validation rejects all executed malformed mode/operand combinations. Optional conditions now reside in the ordinary RHS Filter, so all normal expression visitors see them.

Final public SELECT with empty actual projected domain suppresses iteration before I fallback; internal empty mappings remain valid, including true ASK of the unit mapping. Duplicate public column occurrences survive. ASK uses a contextual first-witness boundary and stops only after an accepted Filter witness. Query-labelled BNODE identity is shared across contextual rows; unlabelled BNODE is fresh per call. Ordinary native per-row labelled BNODE identity is separately preserved.

Governed Group enters and charges the actual Group node, treats incomplete grouped input as opaque, and withholds incomplete accumulators. Row transforms propagate truncated evidence through the existing Lift rather than discarding it. Latched trips stop subsequent callbacks and accumulator admission. Certain rows, aggregate answers, and true ASK answers are checked against actual complete answers/witness call counts across exhaustive small-fuel cutoffs.

## Proportional representation and work

The compiler uses the shared iterative work-list walkers for whole-query census, facts, compilation, expression/EXISTS mapping, clone, and drop. Each source node is summarized and compiled once; source child patterns are moved from a keyed result table, rather than cloned into both branches. Each OPTIONAL RHS and condition is retained once. Per-node domain/context work is bounded by the source's distinct variable width V; mapping/set operations add their ordered-map logarithmic factor. Thus node/context work is O(N V log V), plus source-term/name payload copying and generated alias spelling, and retained generated representation is O(N V). It does not multiply nested OPTIONAL subtrees or repeat whole-subtree variable census at each parent. The ordinary native parser/preparation path never invokes this compiler.

Permanent executed witnesses check optional depths 16/32/64/128, one RHS/condition per source optional, and independent node/variable grids (depth 8/16/32 and width 1/4/16). A private iterative compilation/clone/equality/drop witness runs depth 1024 on 128 KiB. A separate production typed-entry witness prepares and executes depth-32 nested EXISTS on an admitted stack. A stack-guard refusal is not counted as deep-support success.

## Permanent semantic evidence

The fixture asserts that all **241 genuine oracle cases** execute and compares exact ordered sequences or bags as appropriate, including duplicate columns and dataset distinction. Forty-seven capture flags denote callback-capable probes; four ASK controls make zero calls. Six additional BNODE oracle cases execute as typed identity controls, avoiding random-label string comparison. There are thirteen contextual integration tests in total; no skipped/stub operator or zero-case success.

The matrix covers nine original probes and all historical shapes, initialization/collision variants, graph-context/domain fallback, Project/EXISTS, OPTIONAL multiplicity/retry/volatility, all grouping/HAVING/ORDER/Slice laws, nonlazy duplicates, MINUS/DISTINCT, extension relations/SERVICE, SELECT/ASK/CONSTRUCT/DESCRIBE, and successive volatile assignments and aggregate continuations. Configured callback laws execute in Rust through the native registry/VM. The preexisting Python custom-function surface is not executing; these Rust controls do not claim Python callback availability.

The governed group sweeps examine 135/206/80/306 fuel cutoffs across four shapes, including actual computation trips (38/53 on callback shapes), and require Certain aggregate rows to equal complete aggregate rows. Fourteen ASK sweeps require a published Certain(true) to have exactly the number of calls needed for the first accepted witness (0/1/2 as captured); they also preserve false/empty and incomplete outcomes. The matrix, admission controls, RDF 1.2 terms, BNODE identities, proportional/deep controls, and governors are all in the permanent Rust home.

